  "transport_utf8": " The public garden which I visited recently has been transformed with new kinds of trees and plants. The city planners appear to have introduced some European species that are not native to this country. While the changes are interesting, they seem to be out of place in the context of the garden, which previously had a more natural appearance. The removal of certain native plant species to make space for the new introductions has also affected the natural balance of the ecosystem. It remains to be seen how these changes will impact the environment and the way people interact with the garden.\nExperience: I took a walk around the public garden to observe the changes made to the garden and the impact it has on the visitors. I also spoke to a few gardeners and visitors to gain a better understanding of the situation. The experience helped me to think more critically about the importance of preserving the natural balance of ecosystems and the way that design choices affect the environment and people's experiences.\nReflection: As I reflect on my experience, I am reminded of the importance of considering the long-term effects of our actions on the environment. The changes made to the public garden are interesting, but they also affect the natural balance of the ecosystem. It is crucial that we take a more sustainable approach to designing and managing our public spaces, with a focus on preserving and enhancing the natural environment, rather than introducing non-native species. Furthermore, design choices can significantly impact the way people interact with and experience public spaces. It is essential that we prioritize user needs and preferences when designing public spaces, in order to create spaces that are both functional and enjoyable. By considering these factors, we can create public spaces that are both sustainable and enjoyable for everyone.\nA field note about trees in the public garden: The public garden which I visited recently has been